spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:1:# SPEC: Phase 5 — Messaging Resilience
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:5:Status: Final v4 — revised after adversarial reviews r2–r4 
(`reviews/sol-review-spec-r2.md` … `r4.md`); r4 fixes applied without a fifth review by owner decision D7 
(2026-10-06). Approved by the owner 2026-10-06 (native question UI: "Aprovada"). Interview rounds 1–2, confirmation, 
and owner decisions D1–D6 recorded in `state.md` (Verdict: Ready).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:7:## 1. Summary
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:9:The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → 
`MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, state in `MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / 
`useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / `useConversationRealtime`, typing in 
`useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 4 also delivered a failed send that 
keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` idempotency in `POST /api/messages/create`, 
stored in `messages.client_message_id`), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and opt-in desktop notifications for 
DM/group messages. This phase adds: a durable per-user outbox for text messages with server-fenced attempt generations 
and safe cross-tab and cross-account behavior; a connection banner; application-level recovery and health for every 
messaging Realtime stream with polling fallback; a reliable, named, members-only typing indicator; live cross-device 
unread/read sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server 
logs; and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open conversation after reload.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:11:## 2. Problem statement
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:15:## 3. Goals and success metrics
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:17:### Goals
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:18:- G1: A text message the user sends is never silently lost: it 
is delivered, pending, or visibly failed with an action.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:27:### Success metrics
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:28:- With ≤ 10 pending messages and create quota available, all 
pending messages are delivered within 10 s after connectivity returns, without any click, in their original order, 
each exactly once; with more, delivery continues automatically within the server rate limit (D1).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:36:## 4. Users and stakeholders
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:38:### Primary users
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:41:### Secondary users
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:45:### Stakeholders
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:48:## 5. Scope
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:50:### In scope
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:51:- Durable outbox (IndexedDB, per application user, per browser) 
for text messages and replies, written before dispatch; item states; cancel; per-conversation ordered automatic drain 
across tabs; claim leases and recovery; server-fenced attempts; identity fencing; rejection, rate-limit, and 24 h 
expiry handling; session-end and sign-out rules; sending blocked with a clear message when durable storage is 
unavailable (D5).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:62:### Out of scope
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:65:- Editing a pending message (cancel and rewrite instead).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:72:- Other Phase 4 follow-ups: pending-upload cap, toaster 
covering the composer, pin no-op/English copy, orphan storage objects, "Favoritas" discoverability.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:75:## 6. User journeys
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:77:- J1 Offline send: A loses internet and keeps writing; each 
message appears as pending; the banner says "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar". Internet returns; 
with ≤ 10 pending and quota available, every pending message is delivered within 10 s in order and B sees each once; 
with more, delivery continues within the rate limit (D1).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:78:- J2 Reload/close: A has pending messages and reloads while 
offline (the browser shows its normal offline error) or closes the tab. When the page loads again with internet, the 
pending messages are there and are sent.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:79:- J3 Two tabs: A's pending message shows in both tabs as one 
item; only one tab sends a conversation's messages at a time; if that tab closes or hangs mid-send, the other finishes 
them once; cancelling a waiting message in either tab removes it from both.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:80:- J4 Rejection: A's pending message targets a room A has since 
lost access to; it becomes a visible failure with the text kept and "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; later pending 
messages for that room wait; other conversations still deliver.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:89:## 7. Functional requirements
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:93:| FR-001 | Every eligible send (text and/or reply, no 
attachments/voice; online or offline) is first written durably to the current user's outbox with its 
`clientMessageId`, conversation, content, reply target, and requested mention IDs, and only then dispatched and the 
composer cleared. If the durable write fails, nothing is dispatched (FR-033). The item shows in the feed as pending 
while not confirmed by the server. | Must | AC-001, AC-003, AC-040 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:94:| FR-002 | Pending items are sent automatically when 
connectivity returns (browser online, Realtime recovery, or periodic retry), per conversation in creation order, 
always with their own `clientMessageId`. With ≤ 10 pending and quota available, all are delivered within 10 s of 
connectivity returning; with more, delivery continues within the rate limit. A confirmed item becomes the normal sent 
message and leaves the outbox. | Must | AC-002, AC-009, AC-038 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:95:| FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and tab close. Reloading 
while offline shows the browser's normal offline error; when the app loads again, the pending items are shown and 
sent. Items of the current user appear in every tab of the same browser. | Must | AC-004 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:96:| FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order 
is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the 
conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or 
when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after 
fencing (FR-006). When connectivity returns, an attempt that started before or during the outage is superseded 
immediately (fenced and redispatched under a new generation by whichever tab drains), so a hung holder never delays 
the D1 deadline. Across all tabs, no two create requests of the same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | 
Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:99:| FR-007 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic 
attempts for that item; it shows as failed with its text and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later items of the 
same conversation wait until it is resolved; other conversations are unaffected. For `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, 
"Tentar de novo" sends the same content under a new `clientMessageId`. | Must | AC-007, AC-008, AC-043, AC-046 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:100:| FR-008 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the item stays 
pending and is retried automatically, preserving order and without duplicates. The insertion quota is charged only for 
a winning insertion (including among concurrent requests for the same key); replays that return an already-stored 
message do not consume it (D1). Independently, every create request (including replays, validation failures, and quota 
losers) consumes a per-user request budget, and fence/lookup calls consume their own per-user budget; exceeding any 
budget is a transient rate limit. | Must | AC-009 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:102:| FR-010 | Attachments/voice are never queued. A file whose 
upload fails offline keeps the existing per-file error and retry (send stays disabled until uploads finish); a message 
whose uploads succeeded but whose create request fails keeps the Phase 4 composer failure and "Tentar de novo". | Must 
| AC-011 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:105:| FR-013 | When the user clicks Sair at any in-app sign-out 
entry point with N > 0 unsent items, a dialog shows "Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." 
with "Sair mesmo" and "Cancelar". Cancelar produces no logout request, success notice, or navigation. Sair mesmo stops 
drains in all tabs, deletes that user's outbox, and runs the existing sign-out (including the Presence logout sequence 
unchanged). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | Must | AC-013 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:117:| FR-025 | With the Phase 4 desktop-notification toggle on and 
permission granted, a mentioned user receives one desktop notification for a message that mentions them (direct, 
group, room, thread reply), titled "<Autor> mencionou você em <conversa>" with the full text; clicking opens the 
conversation. Before emitting, the app confirms with authoritative data, under the current identity, that the user is 
still a member and (for rooms) mentioned; then, with no further awaits before display, it re-checks that the same user 
is still signed in and that no removal signal for that conversation has arrived; otherwise the pending notification is 
discarded, as it is on any account switch or sign-out. Residual race accepted (D6): a removal that the app has not yet 
received when the final check runs may let that one notification show. Suppression is browser-wide: no tab notifies if 
any of the user's tabs in that browser is visible and viewing that conversation, regardless of which tab receives the 
event first. | Must | AC-025, AC-026, AC-049 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:119:| FR-027 | The client records metric events — send latency 
(press → server confirmed), outbox wait time, send outcomes by reason, Realtime stream drops/recoveries and outage 
duration, polling activations and duration — in a bounded buffer (oldest dropped when full) and sends them 
fire-and-forget in batches of at most 50 events and 16 KB, at most one delivery in flight, each aborted after 5 s, 
dropping batches under overload. The authenticated, per-user rate-limited route rejects oversized requests, accepts 
only closed event/reason enums and bounded numeric fields, drops anything else, adds a server-derived app version, and 
writes structured log lines under BR-013. | Must | AC-028 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:125:| FR-033 | When the outbox cannot be written (site storage 
unavailable or the write fails), nothing is dispatched; the text and reply target stay in the composer with the 
message "Este navegador não permite guardar mensagens; libere o armazenamento do site" (D5). | Must | AC-039 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:126:| FR-034 | Key-based correlation: the sender's representations 
of a message (outbox item, create and fence responses, feed fetches, Realtime events) carry its `clientMessageId`, and 
the feed matches pending items to stored messages only by that key, so each message shows as exactly one bubble in 
every sender tab, including identical texts and Realtime arriving before the HTTP response. | Must | AC-050 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:128:## 8. Business rules
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:132:| BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user 
(`users.id`) on one browser; it is never shown, dispatched, fenced, or deleted while another user is authenticated, 
and the server never stores or fences an item under a sender other than its owner. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A; 
reviews r2 #6, r3 #4 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:133:| BR-002 | Only text messages and replies without 
attachments/voice are queued. | R1-q4 A |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:134:| BR-003 | Per conversation, items are sent in creation order 
with at most one create request in progress across all tabs; a failed (definitive or expired) item blocks later items 
of that conversation until the user retries or deletes it. Conversations are independent. | R1-q5 A, R1-q2 E; review 
r3 #5 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:136:| BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before 
dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client session is valid → retry automatically; unknown = network 
error/timeout/no response after dispatch, lease expiry, HTTP 5xx → fence (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports 
signed out / refresh rejected → hold (FR-012); definitive = 403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT` (incl. a missing conversation), 400 
(e.g. `INVALID_REPLY_TARGET`), 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 409 `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch, and any other 
4xx except 401/429 → failed, no automatic retry; a stale-generation rejection only means a newer attempt exists and is 
ignored (FR-011). | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #2, #11 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:139:| BR-008 | An explicit user sign-out with unsent items 
requires confirmation; confirming deletes that user's outbox; a sign-out not initiated by the user keeps it. 
Cancelling has no side effect. | R2-q5 A, R2-q6 A |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:149:## 9. Data and integrations
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:151:### Data inputs
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:156:### Data outputs
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:157:- Browser IndexedDB outbox keyed by application user ID: 
`clientMessageId`, conversation ID, text, reply target, requested mention user IDs, pressed-send time, delivery-window 
start, order, state (waiting / claimed / unknown / failed + reason / expired), attempt generation, claim holder and 
lease expiry. Deleted per BR-007/BR-008; never shared across users.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:163:### Integrations
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:168:## 10. Constraints and assumptions
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:170:### Constraints
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:181:### Assumptions
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:184:## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:188:| Tab closed right after pressing send, before server 
confirmation | Item is already in the outbox; on next load it is fenced and delivered once (AC-040). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:192:| Hung holder when connectivity returns | Its attempt is 
superseded at once; D1 deadline holds (AC-041). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:200:| Server rate limit during drain; concurrent same-key requests 
| Items stay pending, retried automatically; quota charged once per stored message; replays/fences use a separate 
budget (AC-009). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:202:| Offline attachment / voice | Upload failure: per-file error 
+ retry; create failure after upload: Phase 4 composer failure; nothing in the outbox (AC-011). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:203:| IndexedDB unavailable or write fails | Nothing dispatched; 
text stays in the composer with the storage message (D5) (AC-039). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:208:| Sair with unsent items | Dialog; Cancelar has no side 
effects at any entry point; Sair mesmo stops drains everywhere and deletes the outbox; late responses do not resurrect 
items (AC-013, AC-047). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:223:## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:226:- Every create request consumes a per-user request budget and 
fence/lookup calls their own, so the insertion-quota exemption for replays (D1) cannot be used for unlimited traffic.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:235:## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability 
considerations
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:236:- UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese. The banner text, sign-out 
dialog text, typing phrases, and mention notification title in this SPEC are required; item-state labels ("pendente", 
"enviando", "expirada"), actions ("Cancelar", "Tentar de novo", "Apagar"), are proposed (the storage message in FR-033 
is the owner's D5 wording), and equivalent Portuguese wording with the same meaning is accepted (D4).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:242:## 14. Acceptance criteria
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:244:- AC-001 (FR-001, BR-002): Given A's browser context is 
offline, when A sends a text message, then A's feed shows it as pending with a text label, the composer is empty, the 
outbox holds it, and B does not see it.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:245:- AC-002 (FR-002, BR-003, BR-004): Given A queued 3 messages 
offline in conversation X with quota available, when connectivity returns, then within 10 s and without any click B 
sees exactly those 3 messages, once each, in the original order, and A's items become normal sent messages.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:246:- AC-003 (FR-001, FR-006, BR-005): Given the browser reports 
online but A's create request fails with a network error after dispatch, then the item becomes unknown, is fenced, and 
is delivered exactly once without user action once requests succeed.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:247:- AC-004 (FR-003): Given A has a pending item, when A reloads 
while offline, then the browser's offline error is shown; when the network returns and the app loads again, the item 
is shown as pending and is delivered exactly once. Given A closes the tab with a pending item, then reopening the app 
later delivers it once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:248:- AC-005 (FR-004, BR-003, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A 
has two tabs and queued m1, m2 offline in conversation X in tab 1, then both appear once in tab 2; when both tabs come 
back online simultaneously and m1's real create request is held, then neither tab sends a create request for m2 until 
m1 is resolved; the network logs of both tabs show no overlapping create requests for X; B receives m1 then m2, 
exactly once each; the database holds one row per key.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:250:- AC-007 (FR-007, BR-003, BR-005): Given A queued m1 and m2 in 
room conversation X and m3 in DM Y, and A lost access to X before reconnecting, when connectivity returns, then m1 
shows failed with its text and "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar", m2 stays waiting with no create request sent, and m3 is 
delivered.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:251:- AC-008 (FR-007, BR-003): Given AC-007's state, when A 
regains access to X and clicks "Tentar de novo" on m1, then m1 and then m2 are delivered in order; given instead A 
clicks "Apagar" on m1, then m1 is never delivered and m2 is attempted next.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:252:- AC-009 (FR-002, FR-008, BR-005): Given A's create quota is 
exhausted when A's 12 queued items start draining, then rejected items stay pending and retry automatically, and B 
finally sees all 12 in order, once each. On the real route: a replay of an already-stored message does not reduce the 
insertion quota; replays, validation failures, and quota losers all consume the request budget and are rejected (429) 
once it is exhausted; fence/lookup calls are limited by their own budget; concurrent requests for one new key charge 
the insertion quota once; concurrent distinct keys at the quota boundary store no more messages than the quota allows.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:254:- AC-011 (FR-010, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A adds a 
file, then its upload shows the existing per-file error and retry, Send stays disabled, and nothing enters the outbox; 
given A's uploads succeeded and then the network drops, when A sends, then the message stays in the composer with the 
Phase 4 failure and "Tentar de novo", and nothing enters the outbox.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:255:- AC-012 (FR-011, FR-012, BR-001, BR-005; must-not R1-q16 A): 
Given A has unsent items and A's browser session ends without Sair (auth cookies/storage cleared or refresh token 
revoked so the client reports signed out), when C signs in on the same browser, then C sees none of A's items and no 
create or fence request is sent while C is authenticated; when A signs in again, A's items (< 24 h) are delivered as 
A. Given a 401 while A's client session is valid, then the item stays pending and is retried.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:256:- AC-013 (FR-013, BR-008): Given A has 2 unsent items, when A 
clicks Sair from each in-app entry point, then the dialog shows "Você tem 2 mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas 
mensagens."; Cancelar sends no logout request, shows no notice, and does not navigate; Sair mesmo with a drain in 
flight in another tab stops all drains, deletes the outbox, and signs out with the Presence logout sequence as before; 
after A signs in again the outbox is empty. Given 0 unsent items, Sair signs out without a dialog.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:272:- AC-029 (FR-028): Given the metrics route errors, and 
separately hangs, and separately is flooded with sustained traffic, when A sends, goes offline, and reconnects, then 
AC-001/AC-002 behavior and timing hold, at most one metrics request is in flight from A's tab, a hanging metrics 
request is aborted after 5 s, the client buffer never exceeds its bound (oldest events dropped), and no error is shown.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:279:- AC-036 (BR-015): Each new E2E spec passed 3 consecutive runs 
in Chrome and in Edge; the key flows (outbox, banner, typing, mentions) were verified in Firefox and recorded; no unit 
test was added; every touched unit test was replaced and deleted, recorded in TRACK.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:281:- AC-038 (FR-002, FR-006, BR-004): Given A's create request is 
stored but its response is dropped, then the item becomes unknown, the fence resolves it as delivered without a second 
create, and B sees it once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:282:- AC-039 (FR-033, R1-q2 A, D5): Given IndexedDB is unavailable 
(and separately a failing outbox write), when A sends a text message, then zero create requests are sent, the text and 
reply target stay in the composer, and "Este navegador não permite guardar mensagens; libere o armazenamento do site" 
is shown.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:283:- AC-040 (FR-001): Given A is online and the create request is 
held, when A closes the tab before the server confirms, then on reopening the app the item is fenced and B sees the 
message exactly once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:284:- AC-041 (FR-004): Given tab 1 holds the claim and its create 
request is held, then tab 2 sends no create request for that conversation while tab 1's lease is live; when tab 1 is 
closed (and separately when its lease expires while it hangs), tab 2 fences and the message is stored once. Given tab 
1's attempt hangs across an outage, when connectivity returns with that single item pending and quota available, then 
it is delivered within 10 s (D1) and stored once.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:289:- AC-046 (FR-007, FR-024, BR-005): On the real route: 403 
(missing conversation or not a participant), 400 (invalid reply target), and 413 (content too large) are classified 
definitive in the drawer (failed with text); a 5xx returned after the request reached the server is classified unknown 
and fenced; an unchanged replay after a mentioned recipient left the conversation returns the stored message (not 409) 
and notifies no removed recipient.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:295:## 15. Test strategy
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:299:  - E2E (Playwright, real UI; two or more real accounts; two 
tabs of one account; a second device as a separate browser context): every other AC. Network shaping only — the app, 
routes, and Supabase stay real: browser-context offline mode; blocking only the Realtime WebSocket or a single stream; 
holding a request or response by delayed pass-through to the real server; dropping a response or replacing it with a 
gateway 5xx after pass-through; disabling IndexedDB or failing its writes in the page (AC-039). Old-build 
compatibility: the hosted and Phase 4 builds are run locally against the migrated database (AC-034). Expiry may use a 
test-controlled clock without changing the product rule.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:307:## 16. Validation and launch checklist
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:309:- All Must FRs and AC-001..AC-050 have evidence in TRACK.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:316:## 17. Open questions

 succeeded in 182ms:
   1 // src/lib/messaging/outbox/outbox-db.ts
   2 // Phase 5 T8: IndexedDB storage of the message outbox (SPEC §9).
   3 //
   4 // Database `vo-messaging-outbox` (version 1):
   5 // - store `items`: one OutboxItem per (ownerId, clientMessageId) (key path);
   6 //   index `byConversationOrder` [ownerId, conversationId, order] (the drain
   7 //   reads a conversation's oldest item) and `byOwnerOrder` [ownerId, order];
   8 // - store `meta`: per-owner counter `{ ownerId, nextOrder }` that gives items
   9 //   their creation order inside the same transaction as the item write, so
  10 //   the order is shared by every tab of the browser.
  11 // The connection closes on `versionchange` (an upgrade or a deletion from
  12 // another tab or test is never blocked).
  13 import type { OutboxItem } from '@/types/message-outbox';
  14 
  15 export const OUTBOX_DB_NAME = 'vo-messaging-outbox';
  16 const OUTBOX_DB_VERSION = 1;
  17 const ITEMS_STORE = 'items';
  18 const META_STORE = 'meta';
  19 const BY_CONVERSATION_ORDER = 'byConversationOrder';
  20 const BY_OWNER_ORDER = 'byOwnerOrder';
  21 
  22 interface OwnerMeta {
  23   ownerId: string;
  24   nextOrder: number;
  25 }
  26 
  27 /** The outbox cannot be read or written (storage blocked, quota, failed write) — FR-033. */
  28 export class OutboxStorageError extends Error {
  29   constructor(message: string, readonly cause?: unknown) {
  30     super(message);
  31     this.name = 'OutboxStorageError';
  32   }
  33 }
  34 
  35 export function isOutboxStorageError(error: unknown): error is OutboxStorageError {
  36   return error instanceof OutboxStorageError;
  37 }
  38 
  39 let connection: Promise<IDBDatabase> | null = null;
  40 
  41 function describe(error: unknown): string {
  42   if (error === null || error === undefined) return 'aborted';
  43   if (error instanceof DOMException) return `${error.name}: ${error.message}`;
  44   return error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);
  45 }
  46 
  47 function openDatabase(): Promise<IDBDatabase> {
  48   return new Promise<IDBDatabase>((resolve, reject) => {
  49     let request: IDBOpenDBRequest;
  50     try {
  51       if (typeof indexedDB === 'undefined' || indexedDB === null) {
  52         throw new Error('IndexedDB is not available');
  53       }
  54       request = indexedDB.open(OUTBOX_DB_NAME, OUTBOX_DB_VERSION);
  55     } catch (error) {
  56       reject(new OutboxStorageError(`Outbox unavailable (${describe(error)})`, error));
  57       return;
  58     }
  59     request.onupgradeneeded = () => {
  60       const db = request.result;
  61       if (!db.objectStoreNames.contains(ITEMS_STORE)) {
  62         const items = db.createObjectStore(ITEMS_STORE, { keyPath: ['ownerId', 'clientMessageId'] });
  63         items.createIndex(BY_CONVERSATION_ORDER, ['ownerId', 'conversationId', 'order']);
  64         items.createIndex(BY_OWNER_ORDER, ['ownerId', 'order']);
  65       }
  66       if (!db.objectStoreNames.contains(META_STORE)) {
  67         db.createObjectStore(META_STORE, { keyPath: 'ownerId' });
  68       }
  69     };
  70     request.onblocked = () => {
  71       reject(new OutboxStorageError('Outbox upgrade blocked by another tab'));
  72     };
  73     request.onerror = () => {
  74       reject(new OutboxStorageError(`Outbox unavailable (${describe(request.error)})`, request.error));
  75     };
  76     request.onsuccess = () => {
  77       const db = request.result;
  78       db.onversionchange = () => {
  79         db.close();
  80         connection = null;
  81       };
  82       db.onclose = () => {
  83         connection = null;
  84       };
  85       resolve(db);
  86     };
  87   });
  88 }
  89 
  90 async function database(): Promise<IDBDatabase> {
  91   if (!connection) {
  92     const opening = openDatabase();
  93     connection = opening;
  94     opening.catch(() => {
  95       if (connection === opening) connection = null;
  96     });
  97   }
  98   return connection;
  99 }
 100 
 101 /**
 102  * Runs `work` in one transaction and resolves with its result once the
 103  * transaction COMMITTED (`complete`); rejects with OutboxStorageError if the
 104  * transaction errors or aborts (e.g. QuotaExceededError). Write transactions
 105  * use strict durability (the commit is flushed before `complete`) unless
 106  * `relaxed` is asked for a write whose loss after an OS crash is harmless.
 107  */
 108 async function inTransaction<T>(
 109   mode: IDBTransactionMode,
 110   work: (stores: { items: IDBObjectStore; meta: IDBObjectStore }, done: (value: T) => void) => void,
 111   writeDurability: 'strict' | 'relaxed' = 'strict'
 112 ): Promise<T> {
 113   const db = await database();
 114   return new Promise<T>((resolve, reject) => {
 115     let transaction: IDBTransaction;
 116     try {
 117       transaction = db.transaction([ITEMS_STORE, META_STORE], mode, {
 118         durability: mode === 'readwrite' ? writeDurability : 'default',
 119       });
 120     } catch (error) {
 121       if (connection) connection = null;
 122       reject(new OutboxStorageError(`Outbox transaction failed (${describe(error)})`, error));
 123       return;
 124     }
 125     let result: T | undefined;
 126     let settled = false;
 127     transaction.oncomplete = () => resolve(result as T);
 128     const fail = () => {
 129       reject(new OutboxStorageError(`Outbox transaction failed (${describe(transaction.error)})`, 
transaction.error));
 130     };
 131     transaction.onerror = (event) => {
 132       // The error event reaches the transaction before its abort; reject once.
 133       event.preventDefault();
 134       if (!settled) {
 135         settled = true;
 136         try {
 137           transaction.abort();
 138         } catch {
 139           // Already aborting.
 140         }
 141       }
 142     };
 143     transaction.onabort = fail;
 144     try {
 145       work(
 146         { items: transaction.objectStore(ITEMS_STORE), meta: transaction.objectStore(META_STORE) },
 147         (value) => {
 148           result = value;
 149         }
 150       );
 151     } catch (error) {
 152       try {
 153         transaction.abort();
 154       } catch {
 155         // Already aborting.
 156       }
 157       reject(new OutboxStorageError(`Outbox transaction failed (${describe(error)})`, error));
 158     }
 159   });
 160 }
 161 
 162 /** Adds a new item, giving it the owner's next creation order. Resolves once committed. */
 163 export function addOutboxItem(item: Omit<OutboxItem, 'order'>): Promise<OutboxItem> {
 164   return inTransaction<OutboxItem>('readwrite', ({ items, meta }, done) => {
 165     const metaRequest = meta.get(item.ownerId);
 166     metaRequest.onsuccess = () => {
 167       const current = metaRequest.result as OwnerMeta | undefined;
 168       const order = current?.nextOrder ?? 1;
 169       const stored: OutboxItem = { ...item, order };
 170       meta.put({ ownerId: item.ownerId, nextOrder: order + 1 } satisfies OwnerMeta);
 171       items.add(stored);
 172       done(stored);
 173     };
 174   });
 175 }
 176 
 177 /** The owner's items of one conversation (or of all), in creation order. */
 178 export function listOutboxItems(ownerId: string, conversationId?: string): Promise<OutboxItem[]> {
 179   return inTransaction<OutboxItem[]>('readonly', ({ items }, done) => {
 180     const request = conversationId
 181       ? items
 182           .index(BY_CONVERSATION_ORDER)
 183           .getAll(IDBKeyRange.bound([ownerId, conversationId, -Infinity], [ownerId, conversationId, Infinity]))
 184       : items.index(BY_OWNER_ORDER).getAll(IDBKeyRange.bound([ownerId, -Infinity], [ownerId, Infinity]));
 185     request.onsuccess = () => done(request.result as OutboxItem[]);
 186   });
 187 }
 188 
 189 /**
 190  * Reads the item and replaces it with `change(item)` in one transaction
 191  * (`null` deletes it; `undefined` leaves it). Resolves with the stored result
 192  * (`null` when the item is missing or was deleted). Relaxed durability, see
 193  * claimOldestOutboxItem.
 194  */
 195 export function updateOutboxItem(
 196   ownerId: string,
 197   clientMessageId: string,
 198   change: (item: OutboxItem) => OutboxItem | null | undefined
 199 ): Promise<OutboxItem | null> {
 200   return inTransaction<OutboxItem | null>('readwrite', ({ items }, done) => {
 201     const request = items.get([ownerId, clientMessageId]);
 202     request.onsuccess = () => {
 203       const item = request.result as OutboxItem | undefined;
 204       if (!item) {
 205         done(null);
 206         return;
 207       }
 208       const next = change(item);
 209       if (next === undefined) {
 210         done(item);
 211       } else if (next === null) {
 212         items.delete([ownerId, clientMessageId]);
 213         done(null);
 214       } else {
 215         items.put(next);
 216         done(next);
 217       }
 218     };
 219   }, 'relaxed');
 220 }
 221 
 222 /**
 223  * Removes a confirmed item. Relaxed durability: if the removal were lost (OS
 224  * crash), the item would only be sent again under a newer generation and
 225  * resolved as the already stored message.
 226  */
 227 export function deleteOutboxItem(ownerId: string, clientMessageId: string): Promise<void> {
 228   return inTransaction<void>(
 229     'readwrite',
 230     ({ items }, done) => {
 231       items.delete([ownerId, clientMessageId]);
 232       done(undefined);
 233     },
 234     'relaxed'
 235   );
 236 }
 237 
 238 export type OutboxClaim =
 239   | { kind: 'claimed'; item: OutboxItem }
 240   | { kind: 'empty' }
 241   /** The oldest unresolved item is failed or expired: later items wait (BR-003). */
 242   | { kind: 'blocked'; item: OutboxItem };
 243 
 244 /**
 245  * Atomically takes the conversation's OLDEST item for a new attempt: the next
 246  * generation, state `claimed`, `claimHolder`, lease expiry. Only the oldest
 247  * item is ever eligible (BR-003). The caller must hold the conversation's
 248  * drain lock, so any `claimed` item found here belongs to a holder that is
 249  * gone and is taken over under a newer generation.
 250  *
 251  * Relaxed durability (as for state updates): the write survives a tab or
 252  * browser crash; only an OS crash could lose it, and then the next attempt
 253  * reuses this generation — still exactly once, since the server stores at
 254  * most one message per key and a create first fences older generations.
 255  * Only the item's first write (addOutboxItem) must be flushed (FR-001).
 256  *
 257  * `confirmedKey`: an item of this owner just confirmed by the server, removed
 258  * in the same transaction before the next one is taken.
 259  */
 260 export function claimOldestOutboxItem(
 261   ownerId: string,
 262   conversationId: string,
 263   claimHolder: string,
 264   leaseMs: number,
 265   confirmedKey: string | null = null
 266 ): Promise<OutboxClaim> {
 267   return inTransaction<OutboxClaim>('readwrite', ({ items }, done) => {
 268     if (confirmedKey !== null) items.delete([ownerId, confirmedKey]);
 269     const range = IDBKeyRange.bound([ownerId, conversationId, -Infinity], [ownerId, conversationId, Infinity]);
 270     const request = items.index(BY_CONVERSATION_ORDER).openCursor(range);
 271     request.onsuccess = () => {
 272       const cursor = request.result;
 273       if (!cursor) {
 274         done({ kind: 'empty' });
 275         return;
 276       }
 277       const item = cursor.value as OutboxItem;
 278       if (item.state === 'failed' || item.state === 'expired') {
 279         done({ kind: 'blocked', item });
 280         return;
 281       }
 282       const now = Date.now();
 283       const claimed: OutboxItem = {
 284         ...item,
 285         state: 'claimed',
 286         attemptGeneration: item.attemptGeneration + 1,
 287         claimHolder,
 288         leaseExpiresAt: now + leaseMs,
 289         updatedAt: now,
 290       };
 291       cursor.update(claimed);
 292       done({ kind: 'claimed', item: claimed });
 293     };
 294   }, 'relaxed');
 295 }

