# PLAN: phase-5-messaging-resilience

Spec: ./SPEC.md
Goal: In the production messaging drawer, no message a user sends is lost or duplicated across network and Realtime 
outages, reloads, several tabs, account changes, and several devices; typing, unread/read state, and @mention 
notifications stay live and correct; and messaging health is observable through redacted logs — without regressing 
existing messaging.
Plan version: 4
Highest task ID reserved: T28
Replanned because: owner decision D9 (state.md, 2026-10-07) — the T4 performer found that baseline 
`public.conversation_members` policies/grants let any authenticated user enrol themselves in any conversation or move 
their row to another conversation through the Data API, defeating every members-only rule; owner chose to fix it in 
this phase. Adds T28; v3 preserved as `reviews/PLAN-v3-reviewed.md`.

## Standing rules for every task

- **Migration rule (BR-014, AC-034):** during the phase a migration is done when it is written, applied to the LOCAL 
database, its tests pass, and its rollback SQL is recorded in the migration header and TRACK. Each one gets a row in 
`spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/MIGRATIONS-PENDENTES.md`. Nothing is applied online before T23.
- **Old-build compatibility at introduction (§10):** every task that changes a DB contract or a route proves, with 
integration tests against the migrated local DB, that the hosted pre-Phase-4 client contract (unkeyed creates) and the 
Phase 4 client contract (keyed text/attachment/voice creates and their replays, without the new fields) keep working; 
T21 repeats this as a combined smoke with the real old builds.
- **Online application method (T23 only):** the Phase 4 method (Phase 4 TRACK T2/T25): re-check `npx supabase 
migration list --linked` and the target catalog immediately before; apply only each reviewed file's SQL 
transactionally and record only its version; read back by statement hash plus catalog objects; smoke-check. Never 
`supabase db push`, `migration up --linked`, or broad `migration repair`.
- **Supabase/RLS review gate (AC-035):** every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API route change passes 
the `supabase-rls-reviewer` audit (users.id vs supabase_uid, server client + `auth.getUser()`, service role never 
replacing membership checks) before the task is done.
- **Adversarial review gate (AGENTS.md):** each task's diff gets the cross-model adversarial review (direct Codex CLI, 
`gpt-6.1-sol`, effort `max`, read-only, per `delegation-anthropic.md`) before it is recorded done; findings fixed or 
rejected with reasons in TRACK. The whole work-item diff gets the same review in T22.
- **Test policy (BR-015, AC-036):** integration tests against local Supabase/Realtime/real routes and Playwright E2E 
only. T1–T6 run their regression E2E through the existing local runner (`npm run test:messaging:local:e2e`); from T24 
on, every E2E gate runs through the T24 runner (local Supabase; real Chrome and Edge projects); no unit tests; a 
touched unit test is replaced and deleted, recorded in TRACK. A new E2E spec counts only after 3 consecutive passing 
runs in Chrome and in Edge with zero retries, zero flaky, and no skipped critical test. Network shaping only (SPEC 
§15); no mocked Supabase, repositories, or code under test.
- **Regression (FR-032, AC-033):** every task that changes server or drawer behavior ends with the messaging DB 
integration suite and the messaging regression E2E suite passing locally, plus type-check and lint. Per owner decision 
D10 (state.md, 2026-10-08): per task the regression E2E runs in Chrome only (`--project=regression-chrome`), Chrome + 
Edge at T22 and when a task touches the runner or shared fixtures; a review with only Low findings closes the task 
gate without another round.
- **Presence touch points:** any change near sign-out or Realtime lifecycle follows the `presence-safety` skill; the 
Presence logout sequence is unchanged.
- **Planning decisions under D4 (owner-adjustable):** budget infrastructure fails closed — if a create, fence, or 
metrics budget cannot be checked, the request is refused as a transient 429 (the outbox retries it) instead of being 
allowed; Phase 4 keyed requests that carry no attempt generation keep the Phase 4 keyed contract (create, replay 200, 
409) and take part in the same commit-time exclusion as fencing: such a request commits only if no fence state exists 
for its key at commit time, where fence state is created only by a fence call or a generation-carrying request (a 
generation-less create creates none, so Phase 4 retries are unaffected); a generation-less request that loses to a 
fence stores nothing and is rejected as stale. Per-stream Realtime faults in E2E may inject Phoenix channel frames 
(`phx_error`, `phx_close`) or withhold a single topic's replies on the real socket; this counts as network shaping of 
a single stream (SPEC §15) — the real transport, joins, and application callbacks stay in place and no application 
health is set directly; a real server mechanism is preferred where one exists.

## Observations grounding this plan (2026-10-06, corrected after review r1)

- Local Supabase answers at `127.0.0.1:54321` (HTTP 200). Messaging DB suite `__tests__/messaging-db/*` 
(`vitest.messaging-db.config.mts`) and local E2E (`playwright.messaging-local.config.ts`) exist from Phase 4.
- Create route (`src/app/api/messages/create/route.ts`): `clientMessageId` optional; repository `createWithClientKey` 
dedupes on unique index `(sender_id, conversation_id, client_message_id)` (migration `20261005000414`); the attachment 
path creates through a separate transactional RPC (route.ts:310). The Phase 4 composer sends a key for text, 
attachment, and voice creates (`message-composer.tsx:223`, `useMessages.ts:284`). No attempt generation, fence/lookup 
route, or expected-sender field. Early validation (route.ts:231) runs before any budget is charged.
- Rate limiting: `enforceRateLimit` (`src/lib/auth/rate-limit.ts`) fails open on RPC error (line 48); 
`check_rate_limit` (`20260612190000`) increments a fixed-window counter on every call, including denied ones — an 
attempt counter, not a winning-insert quota.
- `Message` type and feed/Realtime mappings expose no `clientMessageId`; optimistic matching is content/time based. 
Mentions do not exist.
- `mark_conversation_read(uuid, uuid)` is SECURITY DEFINER, executable only by `service_role` since `20261004170724`, 
with no internal membership check; `get_unread_counts` still has default `anon` EXECUTE.
- Typing (`src/hooks/useConversationPresence.ts`): public channel `conversation:{id}`; sends true once and false after 
2 s idle, remote TTL 4 s; payload carries the auth uid and display name; on channel failure it only clears its 
subscribed flag (line 150).
- Realtime: `useMessageSubscription` exposes `connectionStatus` and backoff for the messages channel; 
`useConversationRealtime` exports `connectionStatus` and does catch-up on confirmed streams, but has no application 
backoff and its health is not consumed. `conversation_members` and `message_read_receipts` are already in the Realtime 
publication. No polling. No IndexedDB/BroadcastChannel; Web Locks only in `src/lib/messaging/desktop-notifications.ts`.
- Membership roster: `conversations.participants` is not updated when a `conversation_members` row is removed; browser 
SELECT on `conversation_members` is own-row only; `POST /api/conversations/join` returns 200 from the stale 
`participants` array without restoring membership (join/route.ts:39).
- Drawer (`MessagingContext.tsx`): active conversation ID persisted in global `localStorage` (not per user); open flag 
not persisted.
- Sign-out: the only reachable in-app control is `EnhancedUserMenu` (`enhanced-user-menu.tsx`, rendered by 
`dashboard-header.tsx:81`); the header's own `signOut` handler is unused.
- Test harness: `playwright.config.ts` project `messaging-drawer` uses a filename allowlist of Phase 4 specs and 
`Desktop Chrome` (bundled Chromium, no `chrome`/`msedge` channel); `messaging-desktop-notifications.spec.ts:27` forces 
`channel: 'chromium'`; the E2E fixture signs in two users plus an optional never-signed-in third; the outage helper 
(`drawer-helpers.ts:465`) closes every Realtime socket; the local server's stdout is not captured for tests. Chrome 
and Edge executables are installed.
- None of these contradicts the SPEC.

## T8 — Durable outbox with ordered automatic delivery
Reasoning: The core of G1: write-before-dispatch, persistence across reload/close, ordered automatic drain meeting D1; 
unknown-outcome recovery follows in T27.
Task: Every eligible text/reply send is written to a per-user IndexedDB outbox (key, conversation, content, reply 
target, requested mention IDs, original send time, window start, order, state, generation) before dispatch and before 
the composer clears; pending items render with a text label; items drain automatically per conversation in order when 
connectivity returns, with ≤ 10 pending and quota available all delivered within 10 s; confirmed items become the 
normal message; the outbox survives reload and tab close; attachments/voice are never queued (Phase 4 behavior kept); 
if the outbox write fails or storage is unavailable, nothing is dispatched and the text and reply stay with the D5 
message.
Done when: AC-001, AC-002, AC-004, AC-011, and AC-039 hold in the real UI, and 10 messages queued offline are 
delivered in order, once each, within 10 s of reconnecting.
Verify by: New E2E specs for those ACs plus the 10-message D1 timing case (offline context, reload offline, tab close, 
IndexedDB disabled and failing writes) pass 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and Edge; regression suites pass.
Covers: FR-001, FR-002, FR-003, FR-010, FR-033, BR-002, BR-003, AC-001, AC-002, AC-004, AC-011, AC-039
Root: T8
Depends on: T7

## T27 — Unknown-outcome recovery through the fence
Reasoning: Review r1 #3, #14: fenced recovery is a separate failure surface from durable enqueue, and AC-040 needs it.
Task: An attempt that ends without a usable response (network error after dispatch, timeout, 5xx after reaching the 
server, tab closed mid-request) leaves the item unknown; before any cancel, expiry, or redispatch the app fences it 
under a new generation; a stored result resolves as delivered (never cancelled or expired); a not-stored result only 
permits the transition that was requested — cancel and expiry complete without any create, and redispatch happens only 
while the item is still current, eligible, and inside its delivery window; cancel is offered only for items known not 
stored; a fence answered 403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT` (access lost while the item was unknown) makes the item failed per 
BR-005 with its text kept, stops automatic attempts, and is never treated as not stored (no expiry, no cancel) — 
"Tentar de novo" fences under a new generation first, so a stored result resolves as delivered and a not-stored result 
follows the normal rules, and "Apagar" only removes the local item.
Done when: AC-003, AC-038, AC-040, AC-042, and the AC-046 5xx clause hold in the real UI with real routes, an unknown 
item fenced as not stored after its 24 h window expires without any create request, and an unknown item whose fence 
returns 403 is failed (not expired, not delivered, not re-created) for both a committed-but-response-lost message and 
a not-yet-stored one.
Verify by: New E2E specs (network error after dispatch, dropped response after pass-through, gateway 5xx after 
pass-through, held create + tab close + reopen, stored-but-unknown item at cancel and at 24 h, unknown-but-not-stored 
item at 24 h, via test clock; removal from the room before fencing for a committed-response-lost item and a 
not-yet-stored item, then access restored and "Tentar de novo" → delivered once / sent once) pass 3 consecutive runs 
in Chrome and Edge; regression suites pass.
Covers: FR-005, FR-006, FR-009, BR-005, BR-007, AC-003, AC-038, AC-040, AC-042, AC-046
Root: T27
Depends on: T8

## T10 — Cross-tab outbox ownership and leases
Reasoning: Multi-tab exactly-once (R1-q16 B) is the riskiest client protocol; proven right after fenced recovery, 
before unrelated features.
Task: All tabs of the same user share the outbox and show each item once; per conversation only the oldest unresolved 
item is eligible and one tab holds an atomic drain claim (lease: request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s) that ends on tab 
close/crash or expiry, after which another tab fences and resumes; when connectivity returns, an attempt started 
before or during the outage is superseded immediately under a new generation without waiting for the timeout or lease; 
no two create requests of one conversation overlap across tabs; cancel is atomic against claims and propagates to 
every tab.
Done when: AC-005, AC-006, AC-041, and the AC-045 E2E cases hold with two real tabs, including an attempt started 
during an active outage delivered within 10 s of recovery.
Verify by: New E2E specs (two tabs, held create, tab close, hung lease, attempt dispatched during outage, simultaneous 
online, cancel/claim race, network-log overlap check) pass 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and Edge; regression suites 
pass.
Covers: FR-002, FR-004, FR-005, BR-003, BR-007, AC-005, AC-006, AC-041, AC-045
Root: T10
Depends on: T27

## T9 — Definitive failures, rate-limit retry, and expiry
Reasoning: The user-visible failure states (BR-005 definitive, 429 transient, BR-006 expiry) are a separate outcome 
from unknown recovery.
Task: Definitive rejections show the item failed with its text and "Tentar de novo"/"Apagar" and block only later 
items of that conversation; "Apagar" removes it and the next item proceeds; 409 retry resends the content under a new 
key once; 429 and 401-with-valid-session stay pending and retry automatically in order; items fenced as not stored 24 
h after send become "expirada" without auto-send (a fence answered 403 is a definitive failure, never "not stored" — 
T27); manual retry of an expired item keeps the key and the original send time and opens a new 24 h window under the 
normal rules.
Done when: AC-007, AC-008, AC-009 (drawer clause), AC-010, AC-043, and the AC-046 definitive-class clauses hold, and 
an expired item retried manually keeps its original send time in the outbox.
Verify by: New E2E specs (removed room access via T24 re-add fixture, local quota exhaustion with 12 items, 
test-controlled clock for expiry, 409 key reuse, 403/400/413) pass 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and Edge, with an 
outbox inspection for the preserved send time; regression suites pass.
Covers: FR-002, FR-007, FR-008, FR-009, BR-005, BR-006, AC-007, AC-008, AC-009, AC-010, AC-043, AC-046
Root: T9
Depends on: T3, T10

## T11 — Identity fencing and session-end hold
Reasoning: Shared-browser and account-switch safety (R1-q16 A) is its own guarantee across all tabs.
Task: Before every dispatch and fence the authenticated application user must equal the item owner; an identity change 
or sign-out in any tab stops drains in all tabs; late create/fence/lookup responses after a switch, or 
older-generation/cancelled-item responses under the same identity, are discarded without changing any view, item, or 
claim; items are held only when the client auth state reports the session ended and drain after the same user signs in 
again; another user never sees, sends, or deletes them; a 401 with a valid client session stays transient.
Done when: AC-012, AC-044 (client clauses), and AC-047 (switch and same-identity clauses) hold in the real UI.
Verify by: New E2E specs (session cleared/refresh revoked then C signs in, paused drain then switch to C, held real 
create/fence responses released after switch, cancel, or supersede) pass 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and Edge; 
regression suites pass.
Covers: FR-011, FR-012, BR-001, AC-012, AC-044, AC-047
Root: T11
Depends on: T9

## T12 — Sign-out confirmation with unsent messages
Reasoning: User-initiated sign-out deletes the outbox; touches the Presence logout path, so it is isolated.
Task: At every reachable in-app sign-out control (today `EnhancedUserMenu`; any other found when the task starts is 
included), with N > 0 unsent items a dialog shows "Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." 
with "Sair mesmo"/"Cancelar" (focus-trapped, Esc = Cancelar); Cancelar has no logout request, notice, or navigation; 
Sair mesmo stops drains in all tabs, deletes the user's outbox, and runs the existing sign-out with the Presence 
logout sequence unchanged; with N = 0 sign-out is unchanged.
Done when: AC-013 and the AC-047 Sair-mesmo clause hold for every reachable sign-out control, and the dialog is 
keyboard-operable.
Verify by: New E2E spec (each reachable control enumerated in TRACK, a drain in flight in another tab, held responses 
after Sair mesmo, re-login with empty outbox, keyboard operation) passes 3 consecutive runs in Chrome and Edge; 
Presence E2E logout tests pass; presence-safety review recorded.
Covers: FR-013, BR-008, AC-013, AC-037, AC-047
Root: T12
Depends on: T11

## T13 — Realtime stream health, recovery, and connection banner
Reasoning: Polling, typing recovery, and metrics consume one per-stream health model; the banner is its visible output.
Task: The messages/receipts/reactions and conversation-list streams have application-level health and recovery (rejoin 
after error, timeout, or terminal closure with capped exponential backoff; catch-up refetch of the open feed and list 
on recovery), exposing per-stream health for other consumers; the drawer shows "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas 
quando voltar" immediately when the browser is offline and "Reconectando…" when any required stream has been unhealthy 
> 5 s while online, in a polite live region, hiding itself when all are healthy.
Done when: AC-014 and the messages/list clauses of AC-015 hold.
